{
 "slug":"purchase-order-vs-invoice","tag":"Purchasing",
 "c1":"#6d28d9","c2":"#a78bfa","oglabel":"Purchasing",
 "ogline1":"Purchase Order","ogline2":"vs Invoice","ogtag":"free business docs, no sign-up",
 "subject":"two business documents, a pen and a laptop on a desk",
 "title":"Purchase Order vs Invoice: What&rsquo;s the Difference?",
 "ogtitle":"Purchase Order vs Invoice",
 "cardh2":"Purchase Order vs Invoice",
 "carddesc":"A purchase order starts an order; an invoice requests payment for it. Here is who issues each, what they contain, and how they work together in a sale.",
 "desc":"Purchase order vs invoice explained: who issues each document, what each contains, the order they happen in, and how PO numbers tie the two together for clean records and faster payment.",
 "lead":"A <b>purchase order</b> and an <b>invoice</b> list the same goods but sit at opposite ends of a sale. The buyer sends a PO to <b>order</b>; the seller sends an invoice to <b>get paid</b>. Here is how they fit together.",
 "sections":[
  {"h2":"Who issues each, and when","html":"<ul><li><b>Purchase order</b> &mdash; issued by the <b>buyer</b> at the start, to request goods or services at an agreed price.</li><li><b>Invoice</b> &mdash; issued by the <b>seller</b> after delivery, to request payment.</li></ul><p>In short: the PO opens the transaction, the invoice closes it.</p>"},
  {"h2":"What each contains","html":"<p>Both show the items, quantities and prices. A PO adds the <b>PO number</b>, delivery details and agreed terms; an invoice adds the <b>invoice number</b>, tax, the total due, and the payment due date. The invoice usually quotes the PO number so the buyer can match them.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Why the PO number matters","html":"<p>Many businesses will not pay an invoice unless it carries a matching <b>PO number</b>. Quoting it lets the buyer&rsquo;s accounts team check the invoice against the order they approved &mdash; a process called <b>matching</b> &mdash; which prevents overbilling and speeds up payment.</p>"},
 ],
 "faq":[
  {"q":"What comes first, a purchase order or an invoice?","a":"The purchase order comes first. The buyer issues it to order goods or services at an agreed price. After delivery, the seller issues an invoice to request payment, usually quoting the PO number."},
  {"q":"Who issues a purchase order?","a":"The buyer issues the purchase order; the seller issues the invoice. The PO represents the buyer's commitment to purchase, while the invoice is the seller's request for payment."},
  {"q":"Should an invoice include the PO number?","a":"Yes, whenever the buyer issued one. Quoting the PO number lets the buyer match your invoice to the approved order, which is often required before they will pay and gets you paid faster."},
 ],
 "related":[{"href":"/en/blog/what-is-a-purchase-order","label":"What is a purchase order?"},{"href":"/en/purchase-order","label":"Create a purchase order"},{"href":"/en/blog/invoice-vs-bill","label":"Invoice vs bill"}],
 "cta_href":"/en/purchase-order","cta_label":"Create a purchase order free",
},
